L134: Island constraints (cite118†Ross, 1967 ) are structural restrictions on dependencies between fillers (wh-words) and gaps (empty syntactic positions). Fillers cannot associate with gaps in complex noun phrases, wh-clauses, and adjunct clauses (see examples (cite107†1 -cite107†1 )), among other locations. Islands constitute classic PoS cases because learners must acquire these complex constraints without access to explicit negative evidence, faced with input that would be compatible with unconstrained dependencies.
L135: However, experimental studies show that children exhibit sensitivity to certain island constraints as early as age 3 (cite98†De Villiers et al., 1990 ; cite101†De Villiers and Roeper, 1995 ; cite100†Goodluck et al., 1992 ; cite99†Hirzel, 2022 ).
L136: ##### Computational Modeling
L137: Island constraints appear in broad-coverage linguistic benchmarks, but performance on island-related items is often averaged with other phenomena and receives limited focused analysis.
L138: Targeted studies report largely positive results: pretrained language models capture various complex island effects (cite119†Chowdhury and Zamparelli, 2018 ; cite70†Wilcox et al., 2024 ; cite120†Howitt et al., 2024 ) but fail to show sensitivity to known exceptions to islands, such as parasitic gaps and across-the-board movement (cite121†Lan et al., 2024 ).
L139: Recent experiments with models trained on human-scale data (cite122†Chang et al., 2025 ) further show that models trained on 10–100M words of babyLM data fail to acquire wh- and adjunct islands.
L140: #### 2.2.3 Binding
L141: ##### Definition & Human Evidence
L142: Under Principle A of Binding Theory (cite123†Chomsky, 1993 ), an anaphor (e.g., herself) must be locally bound by an antecedent that c-commands it as shown in (cite107†1 –cite107†1 ). However, disambiguating evidence, i.e., sentences with a reflexive and multiple potential antecedents, including illicit ones, is extremely rare in CDS (approximately 0.07%; see cite48†Appendix C ).
L143: Yet children reach near-adult accuracy by around age 6 (cite104†Chien and Wexler, 1990 ), with even earlier mastery reported cross-linguistically (cite124†McKee, 1992 ; cite125†Emond and Shi, 2025 ).
L144: ##### Computational Modeling
L145: 
L146: Although binding is included in several syntactic benchmarks, there has been little work testing whether models can generalize Principle A from indirect evidence alone.
L147: 
L148: #### 2.2.4 Wanna-Contraction
L149: ##### Definition & Human Evidence
L150: In English, want to cannot contract to wanna when the subject of the infinitival clause has been extracted (Who do you want _ to go? vs. *Who do you wanna go?), though it is permitted when extraction targets the object of want (Who do you want to kiss _? $\rightarrow$ Who do you wanna kiss _?). Crucially, there is no direct negative evidence indicating the ungrammaticality of the former pattern.
L151: Nevertheless, children show adult-like acceptability judgments by age 3;11 (cite105†Hwang, 2024 ), although production errors may persist beyond comprehension (cite126†Getz, 2019 ; cite127†Zukowski and Larsen, 2011 ). To our knowledge, no computational studies have directly modeled this alternation.
L152: ### 2.3 Summary
L153: 
L154: To summarize, prior work has largely studied PoS phenomena in isolation using inconsistent training and evaluation schemes. The lack of a unified framework precludes direct comparison across phenomena, leaving the validity of the PoS hypothesis for neural models an open question.
L155: 
L156: ## 3 PoSH-Bench: A Training and Evaluation Suite
L157: 
L158: Our benchmark comprises two components: a training dataset and an evaluation suite.
L159: ### 3.1 Training Data
L160: PoSH-Bench provides developmentally realistic training input that approximates both the quantity and genres of linguistic experience available to children. Empirical estimates of children’s cumulative exposure by age 5 vary substantially across studies (15–55M in cite128†Hart and Risley, 1992 ; 10–35M in cite129†Gilkerson et al., 2017 ; and 6–60M in cite72†Frank, 2023 ).
L161: Balancing these findings, we construct three developmentally motivated scales, i.e., 10M (lower bound), 30M (midrange), and 50M (upper bound), plus a 100M-word extension representing early adolescence (cite130†MacWhinney, 2000 ). Prior work examines training data size and language modeling quality (cite131†Zhang et al., 2021 ; cite132†Hu et al., 2020 ), but misses the 10–50M range or relies on adult-directed data.
L162: Children’s linguistic input is not limited to CDS: they also overhear conversations (cite133†Casillas et al., 2020 ; cite134†Cristia et al., 2019 ; cite135†Floor and Akhtar, 2006 ; cite136†Akhtar, 2005 ), television programs (cite137†Linebarger and Walker, 2005 ), and others (cite138†Montag et al., 2015 ). We therefore sample a broad range of speech transcriptions rather than restricting to strictly child-directed utterances. Transcribed speech makes up roughly 76% of the datasets from 10M to 50M.
L163: The remaining portion draws from simplified written sources (e.g., Simple English and TinyStories (cite139†Eldan and Li, 2023 )) to approximate shared reading and media exposure.^{5}^{5} 5 The 100M split is constructed from the BabyLM 2025 corpus (cite75†Charpentier et al., 2025 ), excluding children’s own utterances in CHILDES and replacing them with TinyStories. TinyStories consists of AI-generated short narratives designed to approximate simplified written input (cite139†Eldan and Li, 2023 ).
L164: As a result, the 100M split contains a higher proportion of written text than the 10M–50M splits, reflecting later stages of language exposure. To maximize the proportion of speech text in the 10-50M training data, we did not use the BabyLM data (cite75†Charpentier et al., 2025 , 58% speech;).
